A mount fills a table slot that holds a table already,
so the node of the table it held stays in its ring, a slot that no longer is what the ring says,
and a revoke or a destroy that comes to it clears the new table instead.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: slot is not derived from its parent

diff --git a/kernel/process.c b/kernel/process.c
--- a/kernel/process.c
+++ b/kernel/process.c
@@ -148,9 +148,6 @@ int process_mount(struct process *proc, uint32_t index, const struct cap *table,
     if (index >= PROCESS_TABLES) {
         return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
     }
-    if (proc->tables[index].type != CAP_NONE) {
-        return KERR_SLOT_IN_USE;
-    }
     /* It hangs below the capability it was mounted with, as the first hangs below the one the process was made with. */
     proc->tables[index] = *table;
     cap_attach(parent, &proc->tables[index]);
